{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Alan Ojiig Corbiere's presentation started before the Membership Law and before Canada made the list. It started with how Anishinaabe people knew who they were related to and where they belonged.</p>

  <p>Alan did not bring a draft law on August 11. He talked about what membership meant before Canada turned it into paperwork.</p>

  <p>For about an hour, he spoke about Anishinaabemowin, names, doodem, old stories, treaty marks, church records and families moving across the land. His point was simple: belonging came from relationships long before it came from a government list.</p>

  <h2>Before the list, there were relationships</h2>

  <p>Alan began with how a stranger became a relative. He told a story about an eagle giving names. Those names carried a relationship forward. A name did more than identify one person. It connected people to those who came before and those who would come after.</p>

  <p>A government list asks whether a name appears in the right place. The older way asks who knows the person, where their family comes from and whether that connection is still alive.</p>

  <div class="news-article__plain">
    <strong>What the law must decide</strong>
    <p>Does the list decide who belongs, or is the list only one record of relationships that already exist?</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Doodem helped people know where they belonged</h2>

  <p>Alan spoke about doodem as something people used. It helped our people recognize relatives across communities. It could tell them how they were connected and what they owed one another. A person travelling far from home could still find food, shelter and help.</p>

  <p>He showed family stories beside old petitions and other records marked with animals. He read those records as showing doodem passing through the father's line. Doodem was not just a symbol on a page. It helped people know who they were.</p>

  <p>A Membership Law based only on government documents would miss some of this. If doodem and oral knowledge can help show where someone belongs, the law has to say who can speak to that knowledge and how disagreements will be settled.</p>

  <h2>The territory was larger than the reserve</h2>

  <p>Alan described communities as places people returned to. Families moved through a wider territory. They came together for certain seasons and reasons, then spread out again. The reserve was part of that life. It was not the edge of where people belonged.</p>

  <p>He rejected the idea that reserves were scraps left behind. He explained that our people reserved those places for themselves. The wider territory was still shared.</p>

  <p>This matters for members and descendants who live away from Sagamok. Living somewhere else does not mean the relationship ended. Some people left for work, housing or marriage. Others were pushed out by rules they did not make.</p>

  <h2>Canada turned belonging into paperwork</h2>

  <p>Near the end, Alan showed an 1884 record about two women. One woman from the band married an American Indian. She still received her Robinson Treaty payment, but her children did not. Another woman married a white man and was not entitled to a treaty payment. Officials were using marriage to decide whether women and their children counted.</p>

  <p>Canada later wrote those rules into the Indian Act. A woman could lose membership in her own band when she married. If she married a man from another First Nation, she could be moved to his band list. Canada did not separate Indian status from band membership until 1985.</p>

  <p>Sagamok now controls its own membership list. Canada no longer has the final word on who belongs to Sagamok. Sagamok now has to decide whether and how people pushed out by Canada's rules, and their descendants, can come home.</p>

  <h2>Old records do not tell the whole story</h2>

  <p>Alan showed why old records matter and why they can be wrong. A treaty mark may have been labelled by someone who did not understand it. A symbol may have been turned upside down. Church records kept some births, marriages and deaths, but left out families who were not baptized.</p>

  <p>Language and oral knowledge can correct a written record. Written records can help confirm a family story. We need both.</p>

  <p>If oral history, names or doodem can help someone come home, the law has to say how that knowledge will be heard and protected. If only government and church records count, some families may disappear from the story again.</p>

  <section class="news-article__questions">
    <h2>What the draft will have to answer</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Can family ties, doodem, names and oral history show where someone belongs when government records are missing?</li>
      <li>Can women pushed out by the old marriage rules, and their descendants, come home?</li>
      <li>How will the law recognize people who still belong to Sagamok but live somewhere else?</li>
      <li>Who decides when an old document and community knowledge do not agree?</li>
      <li>Who will control the recordings, family histories, stories and other knowledge collected here?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <h2>What Alan brought to the work</h2>

  <p>Alan's presentation was about the world the Membership Law has to come from. He showed how names, doodem, family, language and the land held people together before Canada reduced belonging to lists and rules.</p>

  <p>Sagamok is not starting with Canada's paperwork. We have our own ways of knowing who we are, who we are related to and where we belong.</p>

  <p>The draft will show whether those ways were carried into the law.</p>
